{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Then Judah approached him and said, Please, my lord.</b> Since the brothers had already said to him, ״הָאֱלֹהִים מָצָא עֲוֹן עֲבָדֶיךָ״ — meaning, the creditor found an opening to collect his debt — and they hinted to him that all of this was a frame-up and the Elohim had brought all these happenings upon them on account of another sin they had committed, namely what they did to their brother Joseph, as it says, ״אֲבָל אֲשֵׁמִים אֲנַחְנוּ עַל אָחִינוּ״ and so forth (Genesis 42:21) — regarding that sin Judah spoke as one confessing his transgression and said, ״בִּי אֲדֹנִי״: that other sin, which we suppose is the cause of all the happenings that have befallen us, hangs upon me more than upon any of my brothers. Therefore I am compelled to enter into the thickness of the beam and to speak before you more than all the rest. And the truth is indeed so, as it says (Genesis 38:1), ״וַיֵּרֶד יְהוּדָה מֵאֵת אֶחָיו״ — Rashi explained: his brothers brought him down from his greatness, saying: You told us to sell him, but had you told us to return him we would have done so." "And on account of two reasons Judah said to Joseph, ״בִּי אֲדֹנִי״ (Genesis 44:18) — that sin hangs upon me. The first is because he came to explain why it is that I step forward to speak before you more than all the rest: because I am implicated in all those tribulations that came upon them on account of that sin. And even though Rashi explained what he meant when he said, ״כִּי עַבְדְּךָ עָרַב״ (Genesis 44:32) and so forth — why it is that I enter the dispute more than the rest of my brothers, etc. — namely, because I bound myself with a strong bond to be banished in both worlds, etc. — this itself is a difficulty: why did he accept upon himself a ban more than the rest of his brothers? Rather, it is because he sensed within himself that he was guilty of that sin which brought about the frame-up of ״מְרַגְּלִים אַתֶּם״ (Genesis 42:9), and that they would not be able to clear themselves of it except by bringing Benjamin. Therefore he needed to accept the ban upon himself, in order that Benjamin would be given into his hand and he would extricate his brothers from the frame-up that he himself had brought upon them, by having told them to sell Joseph. And furthermore, Jacob said to them, ״לֹא יֵרֵד בְּנִי עִמָּכֶם כִּי אָחִיו מֵת וְהוּא לְבַדּוֹ נִשְׁאָר״ (Genesis 42:38) and so forth. Hear from this: if Jacob had not been able to press this claim against them, he would have sent Benjamin immediately. And so it turns out that Judah, by having commanded to sell Joseph, caused that Jacob was unwilling to send Benjamin. And because of this he needed to enter into the thickness of the beam and accept the ban upon himself, and regarding this he said, ״בִּי אֲדֹנִי״." "The second reason is that he wished to say, ״יֵשֶׁב נָא עַבְדְּךָ עֶבֶד תַּחַת הַנַּעַר״ and so forth — and the ruler could press a counter-claim against him: 'Why should the guilty one go free and the innocent one be punished by entering the yoke of slavery for nothing?' Therefore he said, ״בִּי אֲדֹנִי״ — it is true that the sin causing all of them to become slaves hangs upon me more than upon any of them; therefore it is just that the punishment of slavery pronounced upon Benjamin should take effect upon me. And the truth is indeed so, for he caused that Joseph was sold as a slave, whereas for the rest of his brothers the guilt is not so great — and all the more so for Benjamin, who had no share at all in that sin." "<b>Let your servant, I pray, speak a word in the ears of my lord.</b> Because he wanted to say to him that the whole matter of the goblet was a frame-up, therefore he approached him to whisper in his ear so as not to embarrass him — and through this he would provoke wrath." "<b>For you are like Pharaoh.</b> Just as it is fitting to render honor to royalty and one must not speak harshly before the king, for ״חֲמַת מֶלֶךְ מַלְאֲכֵי מָוֶת״ (Proverbs 16:14), so too it is not proper to speak haughtily and let haughty speech issue from one's mouth before all who hear. And from him they learned this, for Joseph said, ״קוּם רְדֹף אַחֲרֵי הָאֲנָשִׁים וְהִשַּׂגְתָּם״ (Genesis 44:4) — specifically, when you overtake them and are close to them, you shall speak the matter of the goblet in their ear, and you shall not cry out against them from afar to shame them as thieves, but between you and them you shall speak to them, so that the Egyptians would know nothing of this. Therefore also in this argument Judah wished to whisper in his ear, and his voice would not be heard by the Egyptians. And he said, ״וְאַל יִחַר אַפְּךָ בְּעַבְדֶּךָ״ (Genesis 44:18), for all anger leads to error and causes my arguments not to enter your ears even if they are sound to the understanding, for anger renders you unable to judge justly. And if you should say: what of it, for if sometimes a certain officer or ruler errs in a matter of judgment, there is one higher than every high one, namely the king — they take the case to the king and he corrects what the ruler got wrong — regarding this he said, ״כִּי כָמוֹךָ כְּפַרְעֹה״: with you the final verdict is as with Pharaoh, and there is no taking the case from you to the king, and from before you my judgment emerges absolutely. Therefore I ask that your eyes see what is straight and that your anger not flare, and then you will not come to error." "And one must examine why he spoke in a concealed manner: ״יְדַבֶּר נָא עַבְדְּךָ דָּבָר״ (Genesis 44:18)? And we may say that since he wanted to say ״יֵשֵׁב עַבְדְּךָ תַּחַת הַנַּעַר״ (Genesis 44:33) — for every matter I am superior to him: in strength, in warfare, and in service — he introduced here yet another matter. For every slave must know how to speak in the presence of kings and to arrange his words so that they enter their ears, when they are set forth with understanding and in proper order. But a slave who does not know how to arrange his words is not fit to be a slave to an important ruler such as a king. Therefore he said, in response to what Joseph said by way of decree: ״הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר נִמְצָא הַגָּבִיעַ בְּיָדוֹ יִהְיֶה לִּי עָבֶד״ (Genesis 44:17) — and he is still a youth and not fit even to speak in your presence. And concerning this he said: put it to the test and examine, ״יְדַבֶּר נָא עַבְדְּךָ״ — the one whom you have chosen for yourself — ״דָּבָר בְּאָזְנֵי אֲדֹנִי״ (Genesis 44:18), in such a manner that ״שֶׁאַל יִחַר אַפְּךָ בְּעַבְדֶּךָ״ (Genesis 44:18). And you will find that his anger will surely burn, for behold ״כָּמוֹךָ כְּפַרְעֹה״ (Genesis 44:18), and to speak before a man of your stature requires a wise and understanding man, not a youth and a boor like him. But in me, my lord, you will find everything that you seek, and not in him, for even speech is not in him — all the more so that he does not know the other procedures of royalty. And there are those who say that he said ״יְדַבֶּר נָא עַבְדְּךָ״ — the one who did the deed of the goblet and placed it in Benjamin's sack — and he will tell all that happened, and that all of this was a fabrication of charges."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {1 "The idiom בעבי הקורה, literally 'through the thickness of the beam,' meaning 'into the thick of things' or 'to force one's way in,' is rendered literally to preserve the physical image."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:49.181704201Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}